  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nWeak passing waves will continue to interact with a gradually \nmoistening environment this afternoon through Monday, as a broader \nupper trough gradually digs over the Northeast. A more\nsignificant low pressure system will then develop over our\nregion Tuesday with increasing potential impacts, which could\nlinger into Wednesday.\n\nThis afternoon-early evening, flow is weak and instability\nlimited. Diurnally-driven showers will be scattered and thunder\nisolated non-severe. Dewpoints have crept up which will lead to\nareas of fog formation tonight-early Monday as cloud cover aloft\ndecreases and allows some radiational cooling.\n\nFor Monday, Precipitable Water Values continue to gradually \nincrease, yet some ridging will also occur aloft ahead of the \nmore significant low approaching from the west. This may limit \ncoverage of diurnal showers-thunderstorms. That said, there will\nstill probably be small shortwaves embedded within the ridge. \nThere will also be modestly more Convective Available Potential \nEnergy, especially in Northeast PA and thus the marginal risk \nfor Severe for the threat of isolated downbursts from cores. \nTiming of modeled shortwave and orographic effects will favor \nPoconos-Catskills coverage-wise for showers and storms. Things \nshould be more scattered to the west.\n\nLocally heavy rainfall cannot totally be ruled out Monday,\nthough recent dry weather should allow our area to generally\ntake initial rains well. However, as additional waves occur \ninto Tuesday it could be more of an issue. Models generally cut\noff an upper low and gradually stack it with the incoming\nsurface low, to cause slow movement of features with elevated\nprecipitable Water values around 1.75 inch Tuesday-Tuesday \nnight. It could even linger into Wednesday. From I-88 southward \nin NY, and from I-81 eastward in PA, Weather Prediction Center \nhas Slight Risk for excessive rainfall and at least Marginal \nRisk for the rest of the area. The forecast has areal averages \nof 1.5-2.5 inches with locally higher amounts for Wyoming \nValley-Poconos-Catskills; more like 1-1.5 inches to the west. \nOrographic enhancements, and slow-movement or training of \nheavier showers, will have to be monitored closely for localized\nflooding potential. For Tuesday-Wednesday, severe risk is more \nlikely to be shunted to our south in the warm sector of the low,\nas things become progressively cooler and stratiform with time \nin our region. Still, a gusty few cells could occur Tuesday in \nNortheast PA before the low translates overhead.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nCutoff stacked lows often take longer than expected to break\ndown. Thus we still have a lingering chance of showers and \nthunderstorms Thursday, especially east of I-81. There is\nuncertainty but these chances could easily go higher given the\ntrend in the latest models to keep the upper low in our region\nfor a longer time. Eventually the low departs in favor ridging\nwhich will bring at least a window of drier and warmer weather\nFriday-Saturday.",
